<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Brazilian military dictatorship</h1><p class="st-meta">1964 CE – 1985 CE · capital Brasília</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The 21-year military regime that ruled Brazil from 1964, combining rapid economic growth with censorship, torture and repression.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The coup of 1964 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-goulart">João Goulart</a> ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/fourth-brazilian-republic">Fourth Brazilian Republic</a>. Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humberto-castelo-branco">Humberto Castelo Branco</a> became the first of five generals who ruled as president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institutional-acts">Institutional Acts</a> gave the regime extraordinary powers, abolished the old parties and created two official ones, ARENA and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-democratic-movement">MDB</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1968 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institutional-act-number-five">Institutional Act Number Five</a> closed Congress, suspended habeas corpus and imposed censorship. Under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-garrastazu-medici">Emílio Garrastazu Médici</a>, the regime used torture and killings against opponents and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, while the 'economic miracle' brought growth of around ten per cent a year. Large projects such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itaipu-dam">Itaipú</a> dam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-amazonian-highway">Trans-Amazonian Highway</a> were launched. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernesto-geisel">Ernesto Geisel</a> began a slow opening in 1974, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-figueiredo">João Figueiredo</a> granted an amnesty in 1979.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diretas-ja">Diretas Já</a> campaign of 1984 demanded direct elections. In January 1985 an electoral college chose the civilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancredo-neves">Tancredo Neves</a>, ending military rule and beginning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">New Republic (Brazil)</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-truth-commission-brazil">National Truth Commission</a> reported on the regime's crimes in 2014.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/brazilian-military-dictatorship.jpg" alt="Brazilian military dictatorship" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: Governo do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Brazil_(1968%E2%80%931992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1964 CE – 1985 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brasília</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Humberto Castelo Branco, Emílio Garrastazu Médici, Ernesto Geisel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fifth Brazilian Republic, Military regime of Brazil</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1974&amp;focus=brazilian-military-dictatorship" aria-label="Brazilian military dictatorship on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3374 2765 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Brazilian military dictatorship lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3694 3021L3694 3021M3694 3021L3694 3021"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3694 3021h0M3694 3021h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3694 3021h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3694 3021h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Brasília. <a href="/atlas?year=1974&amp;focus=brazilian-military-dictatorship">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
